# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `apps/room/css/` — NOT evidence. A BUILD INPUT, and the distinction is why it is tracked.
#
# This was a symlink into the capture folder alongside the others above, on the stated grounds that
# "the evidence-bound tests read them by relative path". That was true and it was incomplete:
# `src/app.css` line 1 is `@import '../css/complete-app-styles.css';`, so the file is not read by a
# test, it is COMPILED INTO THE SHIPPED STYLESHEET.
#
# The consequence went unnoticed for as long as it did because it was hidden behind two earlier
# failures. Once lint and type-check went green the room's Build step failed on a clean checkout
# with `[postcss] ENOENT ... '../css/complete-app-styles.css'` — which means the application could
# not be built from its own repository at all. On a fintech app that is a release-blocking defect,
# not a tidiness issue.
#
# Two facts decided it rather than preference. It is ALREADY PUBLISHED: every visitor to the
# deployed site is served this stylesheet, because the build inlines it — so withholding it from
# git protects nothing and only breaks the build. And it is clean: the repository's own detector
# in `apps/room/gate/privacy-utils.mjs` reports no owner name, no gravatar hashes, no addresses and
# no identity payloads across all 8,086 lines.
#
# The capture directory it was symlinked to holds exactly this one file, so nothing else came in
# with it. Everything that IS evidence — `docs/source`, `second-dump` and the rest — stays out, and
# the rule to apply to anything new here is the one this file got wrong: ask whether the build reads
# it, not whether a test does.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `apps/room/scripts/` — every file under it, at the owner's instruction on 2026-08-15: "these
# files should not be on github".
#
# These are the collectors, decoders and e2e probes for the reference-match work. They carry no
# credentials — `ROOM_JWT_SECRET` is read from a local env file at runtime in `lib/enter-room.mjs`,
# `handoff-e2e.mjs`, `role-authority-e2e.mjs` and `room-config-seam-e2e.mjs`, and the `password:`
# literals in the `*-e2e.mjs` probes are per-run fixtures, not real ones. What they do carry is a
# live third-party application: `chat.protradingroom.com` and `cdn1.protradingroom.com` appear in
# them, along with that application's selectors, routes and wire protocol.
#
# This repository is public, which makes that the same republication question already answered for
# `/apps/room/docs/source` above, and it gets the same answer: the artefacts are read from disk
# locally and never published.
#
# A gitignore rule governs UNTRACKED files only, and all 78 of these were tracked when it was
# written, so it was paired with `git rm -r --cached apps/room/scripts` in the same change. The
# files are still on disk and still runnable locally; git has simply stopped seeing them. What this
# does NOT do is remove them from GitHub — 76 of the 78 were already on `origin/main`, and they
# stay readable at those commits until that history is rewritten.
#
# The gate's dependency on this directory was found and dealt with in the same change, recorded here
# so it is not rediscovered as a mystery.
#
# Two files in here were never collectors at all: `verify-privacy-boundary.mjs` and
# `verify-postgres-schema-artifacts.mjs` are the first two of the three commands
# `apps/room/package.json` chains into `test`. Evicting them silently deleted this repository's only
# automated check against personal data entering a PUBLIC repository. They moved to
# `apps/room/gate/`, which IS published, along with `privacy-utils.mjs`, which the first imports.
# The split to keep: collectors that reach the reference application live here and are not
# published; the repository's own gate lives in `gate/` and is.
#
# Seven test files under `src/lib/` read scripts from here. Six of them already could not run on a
# checkout without the off-repo captures, so nothing changed for them. The seventh,
# `authorization-contract.test.ts`, now skips the single case that needs `set-password.mjs` and
# keeps the dead-guard assertion running everywhere.
/apps/room/scripts/

# Release evidence is a BUILD OUTPUT of the backend gate, not source.
#
# `backend-quality.yml` creates `release-evidence/api-<sha>/` to hold the redacted PostgreSQL
# attestation, and `scripts/build-api-release-evidence.sh` then writes the image digest, SBOM and
# vulnerability report beside it — it requires that directory to ALREADY exist (its line 75).
#
# The same script also refuses to run unless the checkout is clean, and it checks with
# `--untracked-files=all`. So the attestation step was guaranteed to dirty the tree that the very
# next step demands be pristine: `[backend:release-artifact] ERROR: release evidence requires a
# clean checkout`, every time, unconditionally.
#
# It went unnoticed because no run had ever got this far — the gate died at an earlier step. It
# first appeared once the attestation started passing on 2026-08-15. Ignoring the directory is the
# fix rather than relaxing the cleanliness check: the check exists to prove the artifact was built
# from exactly the committed revision, and a build output of this run is not evidence against that.
# The directory is uploaded as a CI artifact, which is where it is meant to be read from.
/release-evidence/
